Use `AbortSignal.timeout()`:

```js
try {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000) });
  const data = await res.json();
} catch (err) {
  if (err.name === "TimeoutError") {
    // request took longer than 5s
  } else {
    throw err;
  }
}
```

This works in Node 17.3+ and all current browsers. The timeout covers the whole request including reading the body, so a slow `res.json()` will also reject with `TimeoutError`.

If you also need to cancel manually (e.g. on component unmount), combine signals:

```js
const controller = new AbortController();
fetch(url, {
  signal: AbortSignal.any([controller.signal, AbortSignal.timeout(5000)]),
});
```

For older runtimes without `AbortSignal.timeout`, the fallback is:

```js
const controller = new AbortController();
const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000);
try {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
} finally {
  clearTimeout(timer);
}
```

Here the error is an `AbortError` rather than a `TimeoutError`.
